<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pleissenland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pleissenland was an imperial territory in what is now eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a>, Zwickau and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemnitz">Chemnitz</a>, named after the Pleisse river. Organised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> emperors in the 12th century as a royal domain, it was administered by imperial ministerials such as the Schönburgs. From the 13th century it was pledged to and absorbed by the Wettin margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pleissenland.jpg" alt="Pleissenland" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kooij · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pleissen.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Pleissenland, in Latin Terra Plisensis, was a Reichsgut of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, land held directly by the elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> or Emperor. It lay south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and included the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemnitz">Chemnitz</a>, Zwickau and Leisnig. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry I of Germany</a> conquered the area between 927 and 929 during his campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polabian-slavs">Polabian Slavs</a>, and it became the Gau Plisni within the Saxon Marca Geronis. When imperial authority weakened during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-controversy">Investiture Controversy</a> of the 11th century, its estates passed largely to local comital dynasties, above all the Burgraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and the Margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>.</p><p>Emperor Lothair III reasserted royal claims by staying at the Kaiserpfalz of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a> and by promoting settlement up to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ore-mountains">Ore Mountains</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostsiedlung">Ostsiedlung</a>, including the foundation of the Benedictine abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemnitz">Chemnitz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The territory gained weight under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>. Conrad III, whose elder brother held the Swabian lands of the dynasty, relied on the estates around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a> in his struggle with the Welf duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-proud">Henry the Proud</a>, and set up a permanent burgrave at Altenburg in 1147. His nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> organised the imperial territory, administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ministerialis">ministeriales</a> at Altenburg, Leisnig, Colditz Castle and Lausick, much as he did the neighbouring Egerland, and founded the Imperial city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemnitz">Chemnitz</a> about 1170. His son Henry VI seized the Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> in 1195, but it returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a> on his death two years later.</p><p>During the contest between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-swabia">Philip of Swabia</a> and Otto IV after 1198, Philip secured the Pleissenland by confirming the Wettin margrave Dietrich the Oppressed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>. Frederick II returned from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1211/12, took possession of the estates and established a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> commandry at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altenburg">Altenburg</a>.</p><p>In 1243, in the midst of his conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a>, he granted the Pleissenland as a pledge for the dowry of his daughter Margaret, betrothed to Albert II, son of Margrave Henry III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>. The Wettins confirmed their hold on the couple's marriage in 1255, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> line ended with the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conradin">Conradin</a> in 1268 they rejected attempts by Rudolph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> to recover it. The land remained with the margraves of Meissen, from 1423 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">Electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pleissnerland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From about 1170 they were granted lands in the <b>Pleissenland</b>, the imperial territory around Altenburg, and built up lordships along the Zwickauer Mulde centred on Glauchau, later adding Waldenburg, Lichtenstein and Hartenstein.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordships-of-schonburg">Lordships of Schönburg</a></li></ul></section>
